     NetHack is available in all the comp.sources.games archives,
including uunet.  I don't know whether 3.0 patchlevel 10 is there,
or whether you'd need both 3.0pl9 and the PL10 patch (both of which
are definitely there).

     The 'master' copy of NetHack is on host "linc.cis.upenn.edu",
available via anonymous FTP in directory "pub/NH3.0".  The files there
are in uuencoded, compressed, tarred format.  They can be transfered
as ASCII files, but you'll need Unix-style tools to unpack them.
Those sources include a VMS port, as well as DOS, OS/2, Macintosh,
Amiga, and Atari (and Unix of course).  The microcomputer ports have
binaries in subdirectories of pub/NH3.0.  Note that directories and
filenames on that system are case sensitive.

     NetHack 3.0pl10 (aka 3.0j) is also available in VMS-specific
format, available via anoymous FTP from host "uars.acd.ucar.edu"
in directory [.SRC.NETHACK].  These same files are also accessible
via DECnet on the SPAN network, via ACDURS::SYS$LCL:[SRC.NETHACK].
(If your local configuration doesn't have ACDURS:: defined, it's node
9.367 or 9583:: and the numeric address for uars is '128.117.32.2'.)
See the file NETHACK.README there for complete details.  Basically,
there are two BACKUP savesets, each packaged in VMS text libraries.
One contains all the sources and the other contains prebuilt binaries.
You only need one of those two packages; they're both roughly 3500
blocks (1.75 MBytes).  Rebuilding from sources locally is recommended.

     NetHack 3.0pl10 is also on the Fall'90 DECUS Symposium VAX SIG
Collection Tape, in [rankin.games.nethack...] on volume "VAXB".  The
distribution there is fundamentally the same as all the others, exact
that everything is already unpacked and more-or-less ready to go.

     Finally, 3.0 patchlevel 9 was available via email server.  That
was "experimental", and I've heard no word yet whether patchlevel 10
will be/already is available via the same channel.  Warning: the full
posting of patchlevel 9 to comp.sources.games was 56 parts! so that
should give you some idea of the size involved.

